{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8fb3c74a5d02abf0", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tarXiv:2607.25716v1 [cs.CL] 28 Jul 2026\n2\t\n3\tSpeechLLM Meets Federated Learning for\n4\tEnd-to-End ASR: English and Italian Case Studies\n5\t1st Mohamed Nabih Ali\n6\t\n7\t2nd Daniele Falavigna\n8\t\n9\t3rd Alessio Brutti\n10\t\n11\tCenter of Augmented Intelligence\n12\tFondazione Bruno Kessler\n13\tTrento, Italy\n14\tmnabih@fbk.eu\n15\t\n16\tCenter of Augmented Intelligence\n17\tFondazione Bruno Kessler\n18\tTrento, Italy\n19\tfalavi@fbk.eu\n20\t\n21\tCenter of Augmented Intelligence\n22\tFondazione Bruno Kessler\n23\tTrento, Italy\n24\tbrutti@fbk.eu\n25\t\n26\tAbstract—Federated learning (FL) enables privacy-preserving\n27\ttraining of automatic speech recognition (ASR) systems across\n28\tdistributed data sources, yet its application to large-scale speech\n29\tlanguage models (SpeechLLMs) remains unexplored. This paper presents the first systematic study of federated training\n30\tfor SpeechLLM-based end-to-end ASR systems. We design a\n31\tcommunication-efficient federated optimization strategy tailored\n32\tto the unique challenges of large speech-language architectures,\n33\taddressing high-dimensional parameter spaces, gradient communication overhead, and computational constraints in distributed\n34\tsettings. Through extensive empirical evaluation on monolingual\n35\tASR tasks in English and Italian, we demonstrate the effectiveness and stability of our federated approach compared to\n36\tcentralized training baselines across diverse acoustic conditions\n37\tand speaking styles. Additionally, we conduct a comprehensive\n38\tablation study analyzing the impact of different speech encoder\n39\tarchitectures on monolingual English ASR performance within\n40\tthe federated framework, providing insights into optimal model\n41\tconfigurations for decentralized training. Our results achieve\n42\tcompetitive word error rates while reducing communication\n43\tcosts, establishing practical foundations for federated SpeechLLM deployment in real-world multilingual scenarios.\n44\tIndex Terms—Federated Learning, Automatic Speech Recognition, SpeechLLM\n45\t\n46\tI. I NTRODUCTION\n47\tLarge Language Models (LLMs) have revolutionized artificial intelligence by demonstrating remarkable capabilities\n48\tin language understanding, reasoning, and generation across\n49\ta wide range of applications [1]. Their success has recently\n50\textended beyond text-only processing to multimodal domains,\n51\tincluding speech [2]. In particular, Speech Large Language\n52\tModels (SpeechLLMs) integrate acoustic modeling with the\n53\tpowerful contextual modeling abilities of LLMs, enabling\n54\tunified end-to-end systems for automatic speech recognition (ASR), speech translation, and spoken dialogue. Recent\n55\tframeworks such as SLAM-ASR [3] have demonstrated that\n56\tincorporating LLM-based architectures into speech recognition pipelines can significantly improve robustness, contextual\n57\tawareness, and generalization compared to traditional encoderdecoder or CTC-based systems [4]. These approaches move\n58\ttoward a unified modeling paradigm where speech and text\n59\tare processed within a shared representational space, reducing\n60\tthe need for complex cascaded pipelines and task-specific\n61\tarchitectures.\n62\t\n63\tDespite these advances, the training of SpeechLLM-based\n64\tASR systems typically relies on centralized data collection,\n65\twhere large volumes of speech data are aggregated on remote\n66\tservers [5]. This paradigm raises significant privacy and data\n67\tgovernance concerns, particularly in real-world scenarios involving personal devices, call centers, healthcare applications,\n68\tor multilingual communities [6]. As an example, speech data\n69\tis inherently sensitive, often containing biometric information\n70\tand personal content that may be sensitive for data privacy\n71\tissues [7].\n72\tFederated Learning (FL) offers a promising alternative by\n73\tenabling collaborative model training without transferring raw\n74\tdata to a central server [8]. Instead, client devices compute\n75\tlocal updates that are aggregated globally, preserving data\n76\tlocality and enhancing privacy [9]. Federated Learning has\n77\tshown effectiveness in domains such as mobile keyboard\n78\tprediction and healthcare analytics; however, its application\n79\tto large-scale SpeechLLM-based ASR remains underexplored\n80\t[10]. Challenges, including client heterogeneity, communication efficiency, and model stability, become even more pronounced when scaling to large speech-language architectures\n81\t[11].\n82\tIn this work, we propose a federated training framework\n83\tfor end-to-end SpeechLLM-based ASR. Our approach enables decentralized optimization of SpeechLLM models while\n84\tmaintaining competitive recognition performance. We focus on\n85\tmonolingual settings and conduct comprehensive experiments\n86\tin two languages, English and Italian, to validate the robustness and generality of our method across distinct linguistic\n87\tconditions. The proposed framework addresses key challenges\n88\tin federated speech training, including communication constraints and large model adaptation. Through careful optimization strategies and aggregation mechanisms, we demonstrate\n89\tthat SpeechLLMs can be effectively trained in a federated\n90\tenvironment without sacrificing accuracy.\n91\tThe main contributions of this paper are summarized as\n92\tfollows:\n93\t• We present the first systematic study of federated training\n94\tfor SpeechLLM-based end-to-end ASR.\n95\t• We propose a communication-efficient federated optimization method for large SpeechLLM models that aggregates only trainable parameters on the server.\n96\t\n97\t\fWe introduce a modified FedAvg using a unified exponential learning rate decay.\n98\t• We provide an extensive empirical evaluation on monolingual ASR tasks in English and Italian, demonstrating\n99\tthe effectiveness and stability of the proposed approach.\n100\t• We analyze the impact of different speech encoders on\n101\tMonolingual ASR for the English language as an ablation\n102\tstudy.\n103\t\n104\t•\n105\t\n106\tII. R ELATED W ORK\n107\tIn this section, we review the state of the art, first examining\n108\tFL applied to ASR and related common practices, then discussing the most common communication-efficient approaches\n109\tfor FL.\n110\tA. Federated Learning for ASR\n111\tBeyond the standard hurdles of non-IID and imbalanced\n112\tdata, Federated Learning for ASR is further complicated by the\n113\tintensive resource requirements of contemporary architectures.\n114\tModels such as Transformers [12], Transducers [13], [14], and\n115\tRNNs [15] often impose a computational burden that exceeds\n116\tthe hardware constraints of edge devices. Furthermore, ASR\n117\ttraditionally relies on the massive, centralized datasets [16]\n118\ttypically unavailable at the client level. The resulting scarcity\n119\tof ground-truth labels on client devices necessitates the adoption of unsupervised or self-supervised paradigms.\n120\tThe literature surrounding FL-based ASR has expanded\n121\tsignificantly in recent years. While [17], [18] offer exhaustive\n122\tsurveys of optimization and training strategies, we focus here\n123\ton the most pertinent methodological advancements.\n124\tFoundational work by [19] introduced dynamic gradient\n125\taggregation specifically for ASR tasks. Subsequent studies,\n126\tsuch as [20], have demonstrated that weighting client updates\n127\tby Word Error Rate (WER) yields superior performance compared to traditional loss-based weighting. This research also\n128\thighlights the necessity of a centralized pre-training phase to\n129\tensure convergence, supplemented by a post-aggregation training stage to mitigate model divergence across communication\n130\trounds findings corroborated by [21]. Conversely, [22] suggests that effective cross-domain ASR in an FL environment\n131\tis primarily achievable only through large-scale pre-trained\n132\tmodels, such as Wav2Vec2.0 [23].\n133\tTo counter the lack of local labels, several works [19], [24]\n134\thave explored self-supervised and unsupervised FL frameworks. However, as the present study concentrates on specific\n135\tneural architectures for Efficient Federated Learning (EFL),\n136\twe treat these supervision strategies as orthogonal and applicable to our proposed structural improvements.\n137\tB. Communication Efficient FL\n138\tA wide array of techniques has been developed to optimize\n139\tbandwidth efficiency in distributed systems by minimizing\n140\tthe data volume transmitted to central servers. Sparsification\n141\tmethods, which restrict communication to a strategic subset\n142\tof model updates—such as top-K gradients [25]–[27]—have\n143\t\n144\tbeen successfully adapted for ASR frameworks [24]. Alternatively, Quantization strategies compress local gradients prior to\n145\ttransmission to reduce bit-rate requirements, though this often\n146\tnecessitates a trade-off between communication gains and\n147\tpotential accuracy loss or increased local computation [28]–\n148\t[30]. Furthermore, Knowledge Distillation has emerged as a\n149\tviable solution for reducing exchange overhead by training\n150\tcompact student models to emulate the behavior of highcapacity teacher architectures [31].\n151\tBeyond compression, recent research has shifted toward\n152\tmodifying model parameterization to alleviate resource bottlenecks. Low-rank factorization facilitates the distribution of\n153\tcompact representations to heterogeneous devices for subsequent full-rank global aggregation, a technique explored\n154\tfor CNNs in [32]. Similarly, Early-Exit architectures allow\n155\tclients to transmit only specific model components during the\n156\taggregation process [33], [34].\n157\tRecently, Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) methodologies including Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) [35], [36] and\n158\tvarious adapter modules [37], [38] have proven highly effective for adapting massive pre-trained models like Llama [39],\n159\tGemini [40], and Mistral [41]. Given that PEFT typically\n160\tintroduces only 1%–2% additional parameters relative to the\n161\tbase model [42], it is exceptionally well-suited for environments with stringent computational and bandwidth constraints.\n162\tHowever, it is important to note that PEFT methods can\n163\texhibit higher sensitivity to non-IID data distributions than\n164\tfull fine-tuning, necessitating the use of specialized mitigation\n165\tstrategies [42].\n166\tDespite advances in communication efficiency, current FL\n167\tmethods are largely optimized for smaller, specialized architectures. Thus, it may struggle to scale to SpeechLLMs.\n168\tExisting strategies often face convergence instability when\n169\tapplied to billion-parameter architectures on non-IID speech\n170\tdata. This leads to a ”client drift,” where the global model fails\n171\tto reconcile the diverse acoustic and linguistic profiles across\n172\tdecentralized devices, resulting in poor accuracy and training\n173\tvolatility.\n174\tThis work presents the first systematic study of FL\n175\tfor SpeechLLM-based ASR. We propose a communicationefficient framework that aggregates only trainable parameters,\n176\tsignificantly reducing bandwidth for large-scale models. By introducing a modified FedAvg with unified exponential learning\n177\trate decay, we ensure stable convergence across heterogeneous\n178\tdatasets. Our approach is validated on English and Italian ASR\n179\ttasks, featuring an ablation study on the impact of various\n180\tspeech encoders.\n181\tIII. F EDERATED S PEECH LLM A RCHITECTURE\n182\tThe proposed pipeline, illustrated in Figure 1, consists of\n183\ttwo main components: (a) a conventional SpeechLLM-based\n184\tASR architecture and (b) the federated training pipeline. In\n185\tthe following sections, we discuss in detail each part of the\n186\tproposed pipeline.\n187\t\n188\t\fText\n189\tEmbeddings\n190\t\n191\tProjector\n192\tPrompt\n193\tSpeech Encoder \"Transcribe speech\"\n194\tInput\n195\tSpeech\n196\t\n197\tServer\n198\t\n199\tSpeech\n200\tEmbeddings\n201\t\n202\tLORA\n203\t\n204\tClients\n205\t\n206\tLarge Language Model\n207\t\n208\tAgg = (\n209\t\n210\tclient (1)\n211\t\n212\t(a)\n213\t\n214\t+\n215\t\n216\tclient (2)\n217\t\n218\t+\n219\t\n220\t)/ N\n221\t\n222\tclient (N)\n223\t\n224\t(b)\n225\t\n226\tFig. 1: (a) Conventional Speech-LLM architecture. (b) Proposed federated Speech-LLM training pipeline.\n227\t\n228\tA. SpeechLLM-Based ASR Architecture\n229\t\n230\tC. Large Language Model\n231\t\n232\tAs shown in Figure 1(a), the SpeechLLM architecture integrates three components: a speech encoder, a projection layer,\n233\tand an LLM backbone integrated with LoRA. The speech\n234\tencoder converts raw waveforms into frame-level acoustic\n235\tembeddings. The projection layer then maps these embeddings\n236\tinto the LLM’s token embedding space. Finally, the LLM\n237\tperforms auto-regressive decoding to generate transcriptions.\n238\tFor efficient adaptation, LoRA modules are integrated into the\n239\tLLM layers, allowing only the LoRA and projector parameters\n240\tto be trained while keeping the LLM backbone frozen. In the\n241\tfollowing sections, we briefly explain each component of the\n242\tSpeechLLM pipeline.\n243\t\n244\tIn our pipeline, we rely on TinyLlama-1.1B-Chat-v1.0 [46],\n245\ta compact open-source language model with 1.1 billion parameters, trained on approximately 3 trillion tokens following the\n246\tLlama 2 architecture. Despite its small size, it’s specifically\n247\tfine-tuned for conversational interactions using instructionfollowing and chat datasets. The model is designed for efficient\n248\tdeployment on resource-constrained devices while maintaining\n249\treasonable performance on dialogue tasks, question answering,\n250\tand text generation. TinyLlama uses the same tokenizer and\n251\tarchitecture principles as larger Llama models but offers\n252\tsignificantly faster inference and lower memory requirements,\n253\tmaking it practical for edge computing, mobile applications,\n254\tand scenarios where computational resources are limited [47].\n255\tContemporary LLMs are frequently fine-tuned using LoRA\n256\t[48]. LoRA [49] introduces trainable low-rank matrices into\n257\ttransformer layers to approximate weight updates. For a pretrained weight matrix W ∈ Rd×dk , LoRA represents its\n258\tupdate with a low-rank decomposition W+∆W = W+AB,\n259\twhere A ∈ Rd×r , B ∈ Rr×d are learnable and r ≪ d [50].\n260\t\n261\tB. Speech Encoder\n262\tIn our experiments we investigate the performance of two\n263\tcommon speech encoders ❶ WavLM-large and ❷ Whispermedium.\n264\t❶ WavLM-Large [43] is a self-supervised speech encoder\n265\tdeveloped by Microsoft with 317 million parameters. It uses\n266\tmasked prediction and denoising objectives trained on unlabeled speech data (LibriLight, GigaSpeech, VoxPopuli). The\n267\tmodel excels at extracting robust speech representations that\n268\tcapture both content and speaker characteristics, making it\n269\teffective for downstream tasks like speaker verification, speech\n270\tseparation, and ASR [44]. Its transformer-based architecture\n271\tlearns generalizable features without requiring labeled data.\n272\t❷ Whisper-Medium [45] is OpenAI’s 769-million parameter\n273\tmultilingual speech recognition model trained on 680,000\n274\thours of weakly-supervised data. It uses an encoder-decoder\n275\ttransformer architecture where the encoder processes melspectrograms and the decoder generates text transcriptions.\n276\tWhisper-Medium supports recognition in 99 languages and\n277\ttranslation to English. It demonstrates strong robustness to diverse accents, background noise, and audio quality variations,\n278\tmaking it practical for real-world ASR applications.\n279\t\n280\tD. Projector\n281\tTo align the dimensionality between the speech encoder and\n282\tlanguage model, we use a simple two-stage linear adapter\n283\tnetwork: a projection layer followed by average pooling\n284\t[51]. The projection layer transforms the speech encoder\n285\tembeddings into the 2048-dimensional input space of TinyLlama. Subsequently, we apply 1D average pooling with a\n286\tkernel size and stride of k = 2 along the time axis, which\n287\tcompresses the sequence length by half. This pooling step\n288\treduces computational costs during auto-regressive decoding\n289\twhile consolidating local temporal features into more compact representations. This architecture maintains a minimal\n290\tparameter footprint, concentrating trainable parameters in the\n291\tlinear transformation while the pooling operation provides\n292\tparameter-free compression. During training, only the adapter\n293\tparameters are updated while both the speech encoder and\n294\t\n295\t\flanguage model remain frozen, allowing efficient bridging\n296\tbetween the two pretrained modules.\n297\t\n298\tTABLE I: Statistics (duration in hours, and the number of\n299\tspeakers) of LibriSpeech and MLS datasets.\n300\tLibriSpeech\n301\t\n302\tE. Federated Training Framework\n303\tFigure 1(b) shows the proposed federated learning framework for decentralized SpeechLLM training. Instead of aggregating speech data on a central server, training is performed\n304\tcollaboratively across multiple clients, each holding local\n305\tspeech datasets.\n306\tAt each communication round, the central server broadcasts\n307\tthe current global model parameters to a subset of participating\n308\tclients. Each client performs local training using its private\n309\tspeech data, updating the trainable components of the SpeechLLM (i.e., LoRA and projector layers). The updated local\n310\tmodel parameters are then transmitted back to the server. The\n311\tserver aggregates these parameters using a weighted averaging\n312\tstrategy [52]:\n313\tθ (t+1) =\n314\t\n315\tN\n316\tX\n317\t\n318\tnk\n319\t(t)\n320\tθk\n321\tN\n322\tX\n323\tk=1\n324\tni\n325\t\n326\t(1)\n327\t\n328\ti=1\n329\t\n330\twhere θ (t+1) denotes the updated global model parameters\n331\t(t)\n332\tat communication round t + 1, θ k represents the locally\n333\tupdated model parameters obtained by client k at round t,\n334\tnk is the number of local training samples at client k, N\n335\tis\n336\tthe total number of participating clients in round t, and\n337\tPN\n338\ti=1 ni represents the total number of training samples across\n339\tall participating clients. The aggregated model is redistributed\n340\tin the next communication round.\n341\tBy restricting parameter updates to lightweight adaptation modules, the proposed framework reduces communication costs and improves scalability when training large\n342\tSpeechLLMs in federated environments. Furthermore, since\n343\traw speech data never leaves client devices, the framework\n344\tenhances privacy preservation while enabling collaborative\n345\tlearning across geographically distributed users.\n346\tOverall, the proposed architecture bridges SpeechLLM\n347\tmodeling and federated optimization, enabling privacy-aware,\n348\tdecentralized training for end-to-end ASR systems.\n349\tIV. E XPERIMENTAL S ETUP\n350\tA. Dataset\n351\tFor English, we utilize the LibriSpeech-100 dataset (LS)\n352\t[53], consisting of approximately 100 hours of read English\n353\tspeech from LibriVox audiobooks with high-quality, timealigned transcriptions. We use the train-clean-100 split for\n354\ttraining and the standard test-clean set for evaluation. For Italian, we use the Italian portion of the Multilingual LibriSpeech\n355\t(MLS) corpus [54], comprising several hundred hours of read\n356\tItalian speech with corresponding transcriptions from publicdomain LibriVox audiobooks. This dataset enables evaluation\n357\tof multilingual ASR models in a non-English scenario. Table\n358\tI summarizes the statistics of both datasets in terms of hours\n359\tand speakers.\n360\t\n361\tTrain\n362\tTest\n363\t\n364\tMLS\n365\t\n366\thours\n367\t\n368\t# Spks.\n369\t\n370\thours\n371\t\n372\t# Spks.\n373\t\n374\t100\n375\t5.4\n376\t\n377\t251\n378\t40\n379\t\n380\t247.38\n381\t5.27\n382\t\n383\t65\n384\t10\n385\t\n386\tB. FedAvg with learning rate scheduler (Adaptive FedAvg)\n387\tThe proposed federated learning strategy introduces key\n388\tenhancements over vanilla FedAvg [52], making it well-suited\n389\tfor large-scale speech model adaptation. Rather than assigning\n390\tstatic and heterogeneous learning rates across client subsets,\n391\tthe modified strategy adopts a unified exponential learning rate\n392\tdecay schedule defined as:\n393\tηt = η0 · γ ⌊t/τ ⌋\n394\t\n395\t(2)\n396\t\n397\twhere η0 = 0.001 is the initial learning rate, γ = 0.9 is the\n398\tdecay factor, τ = 10 is the decay period in rounds, and t is\n399\tthe current federated round. This promotes training stability\n400\tand smoother convergence, as clients collectively transition\n401\tfrom aggressive early-round updates to more refined late-round\n402\trefinements.\n403\tC. Federated Learning Setup\n404\tWe implement our FL framework using Flower [55]. We\n405\tdeploy a client for each speaker in the datasets. In each\n406\tround, 30% of the clients are randomly instantiated for 10\n407\tepochs of local training. The resulting gradients are centrally\n408\tagglomerated using FedAvg strategy [52]. FL is applied for\n409\t100 rounds. All FL results marked as —- , —- for LS and\n410\tMLS, respectively, are compared with those achieved with\n411\tcentral training marked as ⋆ , ⋆ for LS and MLS, respectively.\n412\tMore details related to training hyperparameters are publicly\n413\tavailable in our GitHub repository 1 .\n414\tV. E XPERIMENTAL R ESULTS\n415\tA. Adaptive FedAvg\n416\tWe conduct a comparative evaluation of both vanilla and\n417\tadaptive FedAvg on the LS dataset, employing WavLM as the\n418\tspeech encoder. Model performance is quantified using WER\n419\tto assess recognition accuracy rigorously.\n420\tThe WER reported in Figure 2 across 100 federated rounds\n421\tconfirm the superiority of the proposed Adaptive FedAvg\n422\tstrategy. At round 20, Adaptive FedAvg achieves a WER of\n423\t9.7% compared to 19.7% for standard FedAvg with a relative\n424\timprovement of nearly 51%. This demonstrates significantly\n425\tfaster early convergence. By round 100, the proposed strategy\n426\tattains a final WER of 6.4% versus 7.9% for the baseline, approaching the central training reference of 6.1%. These results\n427\tconfirm that the exponential learning rate decay yields both\n428\tfaster convergence and a stronger final model, underscoring\n429\tthe practical value of Adaptive FedAvg for federated speech\n430\tmodel adaptation.\n431\t1 https://github.com/mnabihali/Fed-SpeechLLM\n432\t\n433\t\f100\n434\t\n435\tFedAvg\n436\tAdaptive FedAvg\n437\tCentral Training\n438\t\n439\t100%\n440\t\n441\tWER (%)\n442\t\n443\t80\n444\t60\n445\t\n446\tC. Monolingual FL with Whisper\n447\t\n448\t40\n449\t20\n450\t\n451\t7.9%\n452\t6.4%\n453\t\n454\t0 0\n455\t\n456\tFrom a communication efficiency perspective, both experiments demonstrate that meaningful convergence occurs within\n457\t60-80 federated rounds, with diminishing returns beyond this\n458\tpoint. This finding is practically significant, as it establishes a\n459\treasonable communication budget for federated SpeechLLM\n460\ttraining.\n461\t\n462\t20\n463\t\n464\t40\n465\t\n466\t60\n467\t\n468\tRound\n469\t\n470\t80\n471\t\n472\t6.1%\n473\t\n474\t100\n475\t\n476\tFig. 2: WER comparison between FedAvg and Adaptive\n477\tFedAvg on the LS dataset using WavLM as speech encoder.\n478\tThe dashed line indicates the central training reference (6.1%).\n479\t\n480\tB. Monolingual FL with WavLM\n481\tIn this scenario, we utilize WavLM as a speech encoder for\n482\tthe SpeechLLM architecture for both federated and centralized\n483\tapproaches on LS and MLS monolingual datasets, evaluating\n484\tconvergence behavior over 100 federated rounds. Figure 3\n485\tpresents the WER trajectories, revealing distinct convergence\n486\tpatterns and language-specific challenges in federated speech\n487\trecognition systems.\n488\tIn the case of the LS dataset, as depicted in Figure 3(a), FL\n489\tdemonstrates remarkable convergence characteristics, starting\n490\tfrom an initial WER of ≈ 100% and rapidly improving to\n491\tapproximately 10% by round 20. The steepest performance\n492\tgains occur within the first 20-40 rounds, after which the\n493\tlearning curve stabilizes. By round 100, the FL pipeline\n494\tachieves a WER of 6.4%, effectively matching the centralized\n495\ttraining baseline of approximately 6.1%. This convergence\n496\tdemonstrates that federated optimization can successfully aggregate gradients across distributed clients to reach performance parity with centralized training, despite the challenges\n497\tof non-collocated data and communication constraints.\n498\tRegarding the Italian MLS dataset, as shown in Figure 3(b),\n499\texhibits more challenging federated learning dynamics. While\n500\tthe convergence pattern follows a similar trajectory starting at\n501\t100% WER and rapidly decreasing through early rounds, a\n502\tpersistent performance gap remains throughout training. The\n503\tfederated model achieves approximately 22% WER by round\n504\t100, compared to 20.1% WER for centralized training. This\n505\tabsolute WER difference ≈ 2% highlights the robustness and\n506\tstrong generalization capability of the federated approach,\n507\teven under more challenging data conditions. The languagedependent performance gap between English and Italian federated training highlights the impact of data characteristics on\n508\tfederated optimization effectiveness.\n509\t\n510\tIn this scenario, we investigate the Whisper model as a\n511\tspeech encoder, evaluated under both federated and centralized\n512\toptimization strategies, with the same hyperparameters and\n513\tdatasets as the previous experiment. Figure 4 illustrates the\n514\tWER trends for both monolingual datasets.\n515\tFor LS dataset (Figure 4(a), FL shows rapid convergence,\n516\treducing WER from 100% to approximately 7% by round\n517\t20. The performance then stabilizes, reaching 6.6% at round\n518\t100, closely matching the performance of the centralized\n519\tbaseline of 6.0%. The small absolute gap of 0.6% demonstrates\n520\tthat federated optimization can effectively preserve Whisper’s\n521\tpretrained representations and achieve near-centralized performance.\n522\tFor the MLS Italian dataset (Figure 4(b), convergence\n523\tfollows a similar trend but with a slightly larger gap. The\n524\tfederated model decreases from 100% WER to ≈ 20% within\n525\tthe first 20 rounds and continues improving gradually. By\n526\tround 100, federated training achieves 18.7% WER compared\n527\tto 17.5% under centralized training, resulting in a modest 1.2%\n528\tabsolute difference.\n529\tOverall, the results indicate that Whisper enables stable\n530\tand efficient federated fine-tuning, substantially reducing the\n531\tcentralized–federated performance gap. Moreover, most performance gains occur within the first 40–60 rounds, suggesting\n532\tthat effective convergence can be achieved with a limited\n533\tcommunication budget.\n534\tCompared to WavLM, the Whisper-based federated model\n535\texhibits greater robustness to client heterogeneity and sustains\n536\ta consistently smaller performance gap relative to centralized training, particularly on the MLS dataset. This suggests\n537\tthat Whisper’s pretrained representations and stronger crosslingual generalization capabilities better accommodate the\n538\tstatistical variability inherent in MLS, leading to more stable\n539\tconvergence and enhanced performance under decentralized\n540\toptimization settings.\n541\tD. SpeechLLM versus PEFT\n542\tTable 2 highlights the impact of model parameterization on\n543\toptimization stability and performance under centralized and\n544\tFL using the LS dataset. While full fine-tuning of WavLM\n545\t(85.1M parameters) achieves the best centralized WER (4.4%),\n546\tthis approach requires updating all model parameters and\n547\tis therefore ill-suited for federated settings due to excessive\n548\tcommunication cost and optimization instability. In contrast,\n549\tparameter-efficient methods reduce the number of trainable parameters by over 89% (EL-adapters: 9.1M) and 90% (SpeechLLM: 8.4M) relative to full fine-tuning, while maintaining\n550\tcompetitive recognition accuracy.\n551\t\n552\t\f100\n553\t\n554\t100%\n555\t\n556\tFederated Learning\n557\tCentral Training\n558\t\n559\t60\n560\t40\n561\t20\n562\t20\n563\t\n564\t40\n565\t\n566\t60\n567\t\n568\tRound\n569\t\n570\t80\n571\t\n572\tFederated Learning\n573\tCentral Training\n574\t\n575\t60\n576\t40\n577\t22.6%\n578\t\n579\t20\n580\t\n581\t6.4%\n582\t\n583\t0 0\n584\t\n585\t100%\n586\t\n587\t80\n588\t\n589\tWER (%)\n590\t\n591\t80\n592\t\n593\tWER (%)\n594\t\n595\t100\n596\t\n597\t20.1%\n598\t\n599\t0 0\n600\t\n601\t6.1%\n602\t\n603\t100\n604\t\n605\t20\n606\t\n607\t(a)\n608\t\n609\t40\n610\t\n611\t60\n612\t\n613\tRound\n614\t\n615\t80\n616\t\n617\t100\n618\t\n619\t(b)\n620\t\n621\tFig. 3: WER in Monolingual scenario with SpeechLLM in both central and Federated scenarios using WavLM as speech\n622\tencoder. (a) LS English dataset. (b) MLS Italian dataset.\n623\t\n624\t100\n625\t\n626\t100%\n627\t\n628\tFederated Learning\n629\tCentral Training\n630\t\n631\t60\n632\t40\n633\t20\n634\t0 0\n635\t\n636\t6.6%\n637\t\n638\t20\n639\t\n640\t40\n641\t\n642\t60\n643\t\n644\tRound\n645\t\n646\t80\n647\t\n648\t100%\n649\t\n650\tFederated Learning\n651\tCentral Training\n652\t\n653\t80\n654\t\n655\tWER (%)\n656\t\n657\t80\n658\t\n659\tWER (%)\n660\t\n661\t100\n662\t\n663\t6.0%\n664\t\n665\t100\n666\t\n667\t(a)\n668\t\n669\t60\n670\t40\n671\t18.7%\n672\t\n673\t20\n674\t0 0\n675\t\n676\t17.5%\n677\t\n678\t20\n679\t\n680\t40\n681\t\n682\t60\n683\t\n684\tRound\n685\t\n686\t80\n687\t\n688\t100\n689\t\n690\t(b)\n691\t\n692\tFig. 4: WER in Monolingual scenario with SpeechLLM in both central and Federated scenarios using Whisper as speech\n693\tencoder. (a) LS English dataset. (b) MLS Italian dataset.\n694\t\n695\tIn the federated regime, full WavLM fine-tuning fails\n696\tto converge, confirming the difficulty of optimizing large\n697\tacoustic models under non-IID data distributions and limited client communication. Adapter-based approaches remain\n698\tviable, with federated adapters achieving a WER of 6.1%.\n699\tNotably, Speech-LLM exhibits stable convergence and attains a comparable WER of 6.4% while updating 90.1%\n700\tfewer parameters than WavLM-FT. This demonstrates that\n701\tthe modular speech–language architecture of Speech-LLM is\n702\twell aligned with federated optimization, enabling effective\n703\tparameter aggregation without sacrificing robustness.\n704\tOverall, these results suggest that LLM-augmented speech\n705\tmodels, when combined with parameter-efficient training\n706\t\n707\tstrategies, can significantly mitigate the communication and\n708\toptimization challenges of federated learning. Speech-LLM\n709\tthus offers a practical trade-off between model expressiveness\n710\tand federated efficiency, narrowing the performance gap between centralized and decentralized ASR while reducing both\n711\ttraining and communication overhead.\n712\tE. From Monolingual to Multilingual\n713\tThis scenario leverages WavLM as the speech encoder using\n714\tall available speakers from both LS and MLS Italian, with a\n715\ttotal of 316 speakers available during training rounds. Figure\n716\t5 illustrates the WER performance across communication\n717\trounds for both datasets. Starting from an untrained initialization with 100% WER, both datsets demonstrate rapid error\n718\t\n719\t\f100\n720\t\n721\t100%\n722\t\n723\tFederated Learning\n724\tCentral Training\n725\t\n726\t60\n727\t40\n728\t20\n729\t\n730\t16.8%\n731\t\n732\t0 0\n733\t\n734\t20\n735\t\n736\t40\n737\t\n738\t60\n739\t\n740\tRound\n741\t\n742\t6.1%\n743\t\n744\t80\n745\t\n746\t100%\n747\t\n748\tFederated Learning\n749\tCentral Training\n750\t\n751\t80\n752\t\n753\tWER (%)\n754\t\n755\t80\n756\t\n757\tWER (%)\n758\t\n759\t100\n760\t\n761\t100\n762\t\n763\t(a)\n764\t\n765\t60\n766\t40\n767\t19.7%\n768\t\n769\t20\n770\t0 0\n771\t\n772\t18.4%\n773\t\n774\t20\n775\t\n776\t40\n777\t\n778\t60\n779\t\n780\tRound\n781\t\n782\t80\n783\t\n784\t100\n785\t\n786\t(b)\n787\t\n788\tFig. 5: WER in Multilingual scenario with SpeechLLM in both central and Federated scenarios using WavLM as speech\n789\tencoder. (a) LS English dataset. (b) MLS Italian dataset.\n790\tTABLE II: Comparison of centralized and federated training\n791\tsetups using PEFT and SpeechLLM approaches.\n792\tTraining\n793\t\n794\tModel\n795\t\n796\t# Params\n797\t\n798\tWER (%)\n799\t\n800\tCentralized\n801\tTraining\n802\t\n803\tWavLM-FT\n804\tWavLM EL-adapters [8]\n805\tSpeech-LLM\n806\t\n807\t85.1 M\n808\t9.1 M\n809\t8.4 M\n810\t\n811\t4.4\n812\t4.6\n813\t6.1\n814\t\n815\tFederated\n816\tLearning\n817\t\n818\tWavLM\n819\tWavLM EL-adapters [8]\n820\tSpeech-LLM\n821\t\n822\t85.1 M\n823\t9.1 M\n824\t8.4 M\n825\t\n826\t6.1\n827\t6.4\n828\t\n829\treduction in early rounds followed by gradual convergence.\n830\tBy round 100, the federated model achieves 16.8% WER on\n831\tLS (Figure 5(a)) and 19.7% WER on MLS Italian (Figure\n832\t5(b)). LibriSpeech exhibits greater variability across rounds\n833\twith notable fluctuations between rounds 20 and 60, while\n834\tMLS Italian demonstrates more stable monotonic convergence.\n835\tCompared to centralized training (6.1% WER on LS and\n836\t18.4.% WER on MLS Italian), the federated approach shows\n837\tperformance gaps of 10.7 percentage points for LibriSpeech\n838\tand a negligible 0.3 percentage points (actually outperforming)\n839\tfor MLS Italian.\n840\tOverall, these results demonstrate effective federated convergence leveraging all available clients, with the multilingual\n841\tmodel achieving near-parity with centralized training on Italian\n842\twhile maintaining a moderate gap on English.\n843\tVI. C ONCLUSION AND F UTURE W ORK\n844\tThis paper presented a systematic study of federated training\n845\tfor SpeechLLM-based end-to-end ASR in both English and\n846\tItalian monolingual settings. By integrating parameter-efficient\n847\tadaptation (LoRA and projector layers) within a federated\n848\tframework, we demonstrated that large speech–language architectures can be effectively optimized without centralized\n849\t\n850\tdata aggregation. The proposed Adaptive FedAvg strategy\n851\timproved convergence stability and reduced the performance\n852\tgap between centralized and federated training. Experimental results showed that federated SpeechLLM achieves nearcentralized performance on LibriSpeech and maintains competitive accuracy on MLS Italian, while significantly reducing\n853\tcommunication overhead by updating only a small fraction\n854\tof model parameters. Furthermore, the comparison between\n855\tWavLM and Whisper encoders highlights the importance\n856\tof pretrained multilingual robustness in federated environments. Overall, our findings establish SpeechLLM combined\n857\twith PEFT as a practical and scalable solution for privacypreserving ASR.\n858\tFuture research will extend this framework toward largescale multilingual and cross-domain federated ASR scenarios\n859\tinvolving more diverse acoustic conditions and highly non-IID\n860\tclient distributions. Incorporating advanced communicationefficient techniques such as gradient compression, adaptive\n861\tclient selection, or hierarchical aggregation could further improve scalability. Additionally, integrating differential privacy\n862\tmechanisms would provide formal privacy guarantees beyond\n863\tdata locality. Exploring personalization strategies, including\n864\tclient-specific adapters or federated continual learning, may\n865\thelp mitigate heterogeneity and close the remaining centralized federated performance gap. Finally, scaling to larger LLM\n866\tbackbones and evaluating real-world on-device deployment\n867\tconstraints will be essential for practical federated SpeechLLM applications.\n868\tR EFERENCES\n869\t[1] M. U. Hadi et al., “Large language models: a comprehensive survey of\n870\tits applications, challenges, limitations, and future prospects,” Authorea\n871\tpreprints, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 1–26, 2023.\n872\t[2] D. Zhang et al., “Mm-llms: Recent advances in multimodal large\n873\tlanguage models,” In Proc. of ACL 2024, pp. 12 401–12 430, 2024.\n874\t\n875\t\f[3] Z. Ma et al., “Speech recognition meets large language model: Benchmarking, models, and exploration,” in Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence, vol. 39, no. 23, 2025, pp. 24 840–24 848.\n876\t[4] J. Yoo et al., “Speechllm: Unified speech and language model for\n877\tenhanced multi-task understanding in low resource settings,” arXiv\n878\tpreprint arXiv:2509.04473, 2025.\n879\t[5] S. Alhumoud, “Asr systems using llms a review,” International Journal\n880\tof Computer Science & Network Security, vol. 25, no. 1, pp. 1–12, 2025.\n881\t[6] Q. Li et al., “Llm-pbe: Assessing data privacy in large language models,”\n882\tarXiv preprint arXiv:2408.12787, 2024.\n883\t[7] L. Cheng, J. Han, and J. Nasirov, “Ethical considerations related to\n884\tpersonal data collection and reuse: Trust and transparency in language\n885\tand speech technologies,” International Journal of Legal Discourse,\n886\tvol. 9, no. 2, pp. 217–235, 2024.\n887\t[8] M. N. Ali, D. Falavigna, and A. Brutti, “Efl-peft: A communication\n888\tefficient federated learning framework using peft sparsification for asr,”\n889\tin In Proc. of ICASSP. IEEE, 2025, pp. 1–5.\n890\t[9] M. Nabih, D. Falavigna, and A. Brutti, “Fed-ee: Federating heterogeneous asr models using early-exit architectures,” in Proceedings of\n891\t3rd Neurips Workshop on Efficient Natural Language and Speech\n892\tProcessing, 2023.\n893\t[10] M. N. Ali, D. Falavigna, and A. Brutti, “Federating dynamic models\n894\tusing early-exit architectures for automatic speech recognition on heterogeneous clients,” Progress in Artificial Intelligence, pp. 1–14, 2025.\n895\t[11] P. Hamedi, R. Razavi-Far, and E. Hallaji, “Federated continual learning:\n896\tConcepts, challenges, and solutions,” Neurocomputing, p. 130844, 2025.\n897\t[12] A. Zeyer, P. Bahar, K. Irie, R. Schlüter, and H. Ney, “A comparison\n898\tof transformer and lstm encoder decoder models for asr,” in Proc. of\n899\tASRU. IEEE, 2019, pp. 8–15.\n900\t[13] T. Moriya, T. Ashihara, H. Sato, K. Matsuura, T. Tanaka, and R. Masumura, “Improving scheduled sampling for neural transducer-based\n901\tasr,” in Proc. of ICASSP. IEEE, 2023, pp. 1–5.\n902\t[14] M. Zeineldeen, J. Xu, C. Lüscher, W. Michel, A. Gerstenberger,\n903\tR. Schlüter, and H. Ney, “Conformer-based hybrid asr system for\n904\tswitchboard dataset,” in Proc. of ICASSP. IEEE, 2022, pp. 7437–7441.\n905\t[15] J. Oruh, S. Viriri, and A. Adegun, “Long short-term memory recurrent\n906\tneural network for automatic speech recognition,” IEEE Access, vol. 10,\n907\tpp. 30 069–30 079, 2022.\n908\t[16] C. Wang et al., “VoxPopuli: A large-scale multilingual speech corpus\n909\tfor representation learning, semi-supervised learning and interpretation,”\n910\tin In Proc. of ACL, Online, 2021, pp. 993–1003.\n911\t[17] S. S. Azam et al., “Importance of smoothness induced by optimizers in\n912\tfl4asr: Towards understanding federated learning for end-to-end asr,” in\n913\tProc. of ASRU. IEEE, 2023, pp. 1–8.\n914\t[18] W. Yu, J. Freiwald, S. Tewes, F. Huennemeyer, and D. Kolossa,\n915\t“Federated learning in ASR: Not as easy as you think,” in Speech\n916\tCommunication; 14th ITG Conference. VDE, 2021, pp. 1–5.\n917\t[19] D. Dimitriadis, R. G. Ken’ichi Kumatani, R. Gmyr, Y. Gaur, and S. E.\n918\tEskimez, “A federated approach in training acoustic models.” in Proc.\n919\tof Interspeech, 2020, pp. 981–985.\n920\t[20] Y. Gao et al., “Federated self-supervised speech representations: Are we\n921\tthere yet?” arXiv preprint arXiv:2204.02804, 2022.\n922\t[21] Y. Gao, T. Parcollet et al., “End-to-end speech recognition from federated acoustic models,” in Proc. of ICASSP. IEEE, 2022, pp. 7227–7231.\n923\t[22] T. Nguyen et al., “Federated learning for ASR based on Wav2vec 2.0,”\n924\tin Proc. of ICASSP. IEEE, 2023, pp. 1–5.\n925\t[23] A. Baevski, Y. Zhou, A. Mohamed, and M. Auli, “wav2vec 2.0:\n926\tA framework for self-supervised learning of speech representations,”\n927\tAdvances in neural information processing systems, vol. 33, pp. 12 449–\n928\t12 460, 2020.\n929\t[24] J. Jia, J. Mahadeokar, W. Zheng, Y. Shangguan, O. Kalinli, and F. Seide,\n930\t“Federated domain adaptation for asr with full self-supervision,” arXiv\n931\tpreprint arXiv:2203.15966, 2022.\n932\t[25] F. Sattler et al., “Robust and communication-efficient federated learning\n933\tfrom non-iid data,” IEEE transactions on neural networks and learning\n934\tsystems, vol. 31, no. 9, pp. 3400–3413, 2019.\n935\t[26] L. Li et al., “To talk or to work: Flexible communication compression\n936\tfor energy efficient federated learning over heterogeneous mobile edge\n937\tdevices,” in Proc. of INFOCOM. IEEE, 2021, pp. 1–10.\n938\t[27] S. U. Stich, J.-B. Cordonnier, and M. Jaggi, “Sparsified SGD with\n939\tmemory,” Advances in neural information processing systems, vol. 31,\n940\t2018.\n941\t[28] N. Tonellotto et al., “Neural network quantization in federated learning\n942\tat the edge,” Information Sciences, vol. 575, pp. 417–436, 2021.\n943\t\n944\t[29] F. Yu et al., “Communication-efficient personalized federated metalearning in edge networks,” IEEE Transactions on Network and Service\n945\tManagement, vol. 20, no. 2, pp. 1558–1571, 2023.\n946\t[30] L. Liu, J. Zhang, S. Song, and K. B. Letaief, “Hierarchical federated\n947\tlearning with quantization: Convergence analysis and system design,”\n948\tIEEE Transactions on Wireless Communications, vol. 22, no. 1, pp. 2–\n949\t18, 2022.\n950\t[31] Z. Zhu, J. Hong, and J. Zhou, “Data-free knowledge distillation for heterogeneous federated learning,” in International conference on machine\n951\tlearning. PMLR, 2021, pp. 12 878–12 889.\n952\t[32] D. Yao et al., “FedHM: Efficient federated learning for heterogeneous\n953\tmodels via low-rank factorization,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2111.14655,\n954\t2021.\n955\t[33] M. N. Ali, A. Brutti, and D. Falavigna, “Federating dynamic models\n956\tusing early-exit architectures for automatic speech recognition on heterogeneous clients,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2405.17376, 2024.\n957\t[34] R. Lee et al., “Recurrent early exits for federated learning with heterogeneous clients,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2405.14791, 2024.\n958\t[35] E. Hu et al., “LoRA: Low-rank adaptation of large language models,”\n959\tin In Proc. of ICLR, 2022.\n960\t[36] Q. Zhang et al., “Adaptive budget allocation for parameter-efficient finetuning,” in ICLR, 2023.\n961\t[37] J. Pfeiffer et al., “Adapterfusion: Non-destructive task composition for\n962\ttransfer learning,” in EACL, 2021.\n963\t[38] N. Houlsby et al., “Parameter-efficient transfer learning for NLP,” in\n964\tICML, 2019.\n965\t[39] H. Touvron et al., “Llama: Open and efficient foundation language\n966\tmodels,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2302.13971, 2023.\n967\t[40] G. Team et al., “Gemini: a family of highly capable multimodal models,”\n968\tarXiv preprint arXiv:2312.11805, 2023.\n969\t[41] A. Q. o. Jiang, “Mistral 7b,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2310.06825, 2023.\n970\t[42] S. Babakniya et al., “SLoRA: Federated parameter efficient fine-tuning\n971\tof language models,” in International Workshop on Federated Learning\n972\tin the Age of Foundation Models, NeurIPS, 2023.\n973\t[43] S. Chen et al., “Wavlm: Large-scale self-supervised pre-training for full\n974\tstack speech processing,” IEEE Journal of Selected Topics in Signal\n975\tProcessing, vol. 16, no. 6, pp. 1505–1518, 2022.\n976\t[44] S. Hu et al., “Wavllm: Towards robust and adaptive speech large\n977\tlanguage model,” in In Proc. of EMNLP 2024, 2024, pp. 4552–4572.\n978\t[45] A. Radford et al., “Robust speech recognition via large-scale weak\n979\tsupervision,” in International conference on machine learning. PMLR,\n980\t2023, pp. 28 492–28 518.\n981\t[46] P. Zhang, G. Zeng, T. Wang, and W. Lu, “Tinyllama: An open-source\n982\tsmall language model,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2401.02385, 2024.\n983\t[47] X. Wang et al., “Efficient and personalized mobile health event prediction via small language models,” in In Proc. of Annual International\n984\tConference on Mobile Computing and Networking, 2024, pp. 2353–\n985\t2358.\n986\t[48] B. Zhang et al., “When scaling meets llm finetuning: The effect of data,\n987\tmodel and finetuning method,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2402.17193, 2024.\n988\t[49] E. J. Hu et al., “LoRA: Low-rank adaptation of large language models,”\n989\tin International Conference on Learning Representations, 2022.\n990\t[Online]. Available: https://openreview.net/forum?id=nZeVKeeFYf9\n991\t[50] S. Pletenev et al., “How much knowledge can you pack into a lora\n992\tadapter without harming llm?” in In Proc. of NAACL, 2025, pp. 4309–\n993\t4322.\n994\t[51] Z. Ma et al., “Slam-llm: A modular, open-source multimodal large\n995\tlanguage model framework and best practice for speech, language,\n996\taudio and music processing,” IEEE Journal of Selected Topics in Signal\n997\tProcessing, 2026.\n998\t[52] B. McMahan et al., “Communication-efficient learning of deep networks\n999\tfrom decentralized data,” in Artificial intelligence and statistics. PMLR,\n1000\t2017, pp. 1273–1282.\n1001\t[53] V. Panayotov et al., “Librispeech: an asr corpus based on public domain\n1002\taudio books,” in In Proc. of ICASSP. IEEE, 2015, pp. 5206–5210.\n1003\t[54] V. Pratap et al., “Mls: A large-scale multilingual dataset for speech\n1004\tresearch,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2012.03411, 2020.\n1005\t[55] D. J. Beutel et al., “Flower: A friendly federated learning research\n1006\tframework,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2007.14390, 2020.\n1007\t\n1008\t\f\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "a8497c6a-48cc-4b4b-bb70-a27be4abbcf9", "uuid": "d3bcd230-df30-49d5-bfeb-3796c52041af", "timestamp": "2026-07-29T10:12:16.961Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_pdf.txt", "content": "arXiv:2607.25716v1 [cs.CL] 28 Jul 2026\n\nSpeechLLM Meets Federated Learning for\nEnd-to-End ASR: English and Italian Case Studies\n1st Mohamed Nabih Ali\n\n2nd Daniele Falavigna\n\n3rd Alessio Brutti\n\nCenter of Augmented Intelligence\nFondazione Bruno Kessler\nTrento, Italy\nmnabih@fbk.eu\n\nCenter of Augmented Intelligence\nFondazione Bruno Kessler\nTrento, Italy\nfalavi@fbk.eu\n\nCenter of Augmented Intelligence\nFondazione Bruno Kessler\nTrento, Italy\nbrutti@fbk.eu\n\nAbstract—Federated learning (FL) enables privacy-preserving\ntraining of automatic speech recognition (ASR) systems across\ndistributed data sources, yet its application to large-scale speech\nlanguage models (SpeechLLMs) remains unexplored. This paper presents the first systematic study of federated training\nfor SpeechLLM-based end-to-end ASR systems. We design a\ncommunication-efficient federated optimization strategy tailored\nto the unique challenges of large speech-language architectures,\naddressing high-dimensional parameter spaces, gradient communication overhead, and computational constraints in distributed\nsettings. Through extensive empirical evaluation on monolingual\nASR tasks in English and Italian, we demonstrate the effectiveness and stability of our federated approach compared to\ncentralized training baselines across diverse acoustic conditions\nand speaking styles. Additionally, we conduct a comprehensive\nablation study analyzing the impact of different speech encoder\narchitectures on monolingual English ASR performance within\nthe federated framework, providing insights into optimal model\nconfigurations for decentralized training. Our results achieve\ncompetitive word error rates while reducing communication\ncosts, establishing practical foundations for federated SpeechLLM deployment in real-world multilingual scenarios.\nIndex Terms—Federated Learning, Automatic Speech Recognition, SpeechLLM\n\nI. I NTRODUCTION\nLarge Language Models (LLMs) have revolutionized artificial intelligence by demonstrating remarkable capabilities\nin language understanding, reasoning, and generation across\na wide range of applications [1]. Their success has recently\nextended beyond text-only processing to multimodal domains,\nincluding speech [2]. In particular, Speech Large Language\nModels (SpeechLLMs) integrate acoustic modeling with the\npowerful contextual modeling abilities of LLMs, enabling\nunified end-to-end systems for automatic speech recognition (ASR), speech translation, and spoken dialogue. Recent\nframeworks such as SLAM-ASR [3] have demonstrated that\nincorporating LLM-based architectures into speech recognition pipelines can significantly improve robustness, contextual\nawareness, and generalization compared to traditional encoderdecoder or CTC-based systems [4]. These approaches move\ntoward a unified modeling paradigm where speech and text\nare processed within a shared representational space, reducing\nthe need for complex cascaded pipelines and task-specific\narchitectures.\n\nDespite these advances, the training of SpeechLLM-based\nASR systems typically relies on centralized data collection,\nwhere large volumes of speech data are aggregated on remote\nservers [5]. This paradigm raises significant privacy and data\ngovernance concerns, particularly in real-world scenarios involving personal devices, call centers, healthcare applications,\nor multilingual communities [6]. As an example, speech data\nis inherently sensitive, often containing biometric information\nand personal content that may be sensitive for data privacy\nissues [7].\nFederated Learning (FL) offers a promising alternative by\nenabling collaborative model training without transferring raw\ndata to a central server [8]. Instead, client devices compute\nlocal updates that are aggregated globally, preserving data\nlocality and enhancing privacy [9]. Federated Learning has\nshown effectiveness in domains such as mobile keyboard\nprediction and healthcare analytics; however, its application\nto large-scale SpeechLLM-based ASR remains underexplored\n[10]. Challenges, including client heterogeneity, communication efficiency, and model stability, become even more pronounced when scaling to large speech-language architectures\n[11].\nIn this work, we propose a federated training framework\nfor end-to-end SpeechLLM-based ASR. Our approach enables decentralized optimization of SpeechLLM models while\nmaintaining competitive recognition performance. We focus on\nmonolingual settings and conduct comprehensive experiments\nin two languages, English and Italian, to validate the robustness and generality of our method across distinct linguistic\nconditions. The proposed framework addresses key challenges\nin federated speech training, including communication constraints and large model adaptation. Through careful optimization strategies and aggregation mechanisms, we demonstrate\nthat SpeechLLMs can be effectively trained in a federated\nenvironment without sacrificing accuracy.\nThe main contributions of this paper are summarized as\nfollows:\n• We present the first systematic study of federated training\nfor SpeechLLM-based end-to-end ASR.\n• We propose a communication-efficient federated optimization method for large SpeechLLM models that aggregates only trainable parameters on the server.\n\n\fWe introduce a modified FedAvg using a unified exponential learning rate decay.\n• We provide an extensive empirical evaluation on monolingual ASR tasks in English and Italian, demonstrating\nthe effectiveness and stability of the proposed approach.\n• We analyze the impact of different speech encoders on\nMonolingual ASR for the English language as an ablation\nstudy.\n\n•\n\nII. R ELATED W ORK\nIn this section, we review the state of the art, first examining\nFL applied to ASR and related common practices, then discussing the most common communication-efficient approaches\nfor FL.\nA. Federated Learning for ASR\nBeyond the standard hurdles of non-IID and imbalanced\ndata, Federated Learning for ASR is further complicated by the\nintensive resource requirements of contemporary architectures.\nModels such as Transformers [12], Transducers [13], [14], and\nRNNs [15] often impose a computational burden that exceeds\nthe hardware constraints of edge devices. Furthermore, ASR\ntraditionally relies on the massive, centralized datasets [16]\ntypically unavailable at the client level. The resulting scarcity\nof ground-truth labels on client devices necessitates the adoption of unsupervised or self-supervised paradigms.\nThe literature surrounding FL-based ASR has expanded\nsignificantly in recent years. While [17], [18] offer exhaustive\nsurveys of optimization and training strategies, we focus here\non the most pertinent methodological advancements.\nFoundational work by [19] introduced dynamic gradient\naggregation specifically for ASR tasks. Subsequent studies,\nsuch as [20], have demonstrated that weighting client updates\nby Word Error Rate (WER) yields superior performance compared to traditional loss-based weighting. This research also\nhighlights the necessity of a centralized pre-training phase to\nensure convergence, supplemented by a post-aggregation training stage to mitigate model divergence across communication\nrounds findings corroborated by [21]. Conversely, [22] suggests that effective cross-domain ASR in an FL environment\nis primarily achievable only through large-scale pre-trained\nmodels, such as Wav2Vec2.0 [23].\nTo counter the lack of local labels, several works [19], [24]\nhave explored self-supervised and unsupervised FL frameworks. However, as the present study concentrates on specific\nneural architectures for Efficient Federated Learning (EFL),\nwe treat these supervision strategies as orthogonal and applicable to our proposed structural improvements.\nB. Communication Efficient FL\nA wide array of techniques has been developed to optimize\nbandwidth efficiency in distributed systems by minimizing\nthe data volume transmitted to central servers. Sparsification\nmethods, which restrict communication to a strategic subset\nof model updates—such as top-K gradients [25]–[27]—have\n\nbeen successfully adapted for ASR frameworks [24]. Alternatively, Quantization strategies compress local gradients prior to\ntransmission to reduce bit-rate requirements, though this often\nnecessitates a trade-off between communication gains and\npotential accuracy loss or increased local computation [28]–\n[30]. Furthermore, Knowledge Distillation has emerged as a\nviable solution for reducing exchange overhead by training\ncompact student models to emulate the behavior of highcapacity teacher architectures [31].\nBeyond compression, recent research has shifted toward\nmodifying model parameterization to alleviate resource bottlenecks. Low-rank factorization facilitates the distribution of\ncompact representations to heterogeneous devices for subsequent full-rank global aggregation, a technique explored\nfor CNNs in [32]. Similarly, Early-Exit architectures allow\nclients to transmit only specific model components during the\naggregation process [33], [34].\nRecently, Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) methodologies including Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) [35], [36] and\nvarious adapter modules [37], [38] have proven highly effective for adapting massive pre-trained models like Llama [39],\nGemini [40], and Mistral [41]. Given that PEFT typically\nintroduces only 1%–2% additional parameters relative to the\nbase model [42], it is exceptionally well-suited for environments with stringent computational and bandwidth constraints.\nHowever, it is important to note that PEFT methods can\nexhibit higher sensitivity to non-IID data distributions than\nfull fine-tuning, necessitating the use of specialized mitigation\nstrategies [42].\nDespite advances in communication efficiency, current FL\nmethods are largely optimized for smaller, specialized architectures. Thus, it may struggle to scale to SpeechLLMs.\nExisting strategies often face convergence instability when\napplied to billion-parameter architectures on non-IID speech\ndata. This leads to a ”client drift,” where the global model fails\nto reconcile the diverse acoustic and linguistic profiles across\ndecentralized devices, resulting in poor accuracy and training\nvolatility.\nThis work presents the first systematic study of FL\nfor SpeechLLM-based ASR. We propose a communicationefficient framework that aggregates only trainable parameters,\nsignificantly reducing bandwidth for large-scale models. By introducing a modified FedAvg with unified exponential learning\nrate decay, we ensure stable convergence across heterogeneous\ndatasets. Our approach is validated on English and Italian ASR\ntasks, featuring an ablation study on the impact of various\nspeech encoders.\nIII. F EDERATED S PEECH LLM A RCHITECTURE\nThe proposed pipeline, illustrated in Figure 1, consists of\ntwo main components: (a) a conventional SpeechLLM-based\nASR architecture and (b) the federated training pipeline. In\nthe following sections, we discuss in detail each part of the\nproposed pipeline.\n\n\fText\nEmbeddings\n\nProjector\nPrompt\nSpeech Encoder \"Transcribe speech\"\nInput\nSpeech\n\nServer\n\nSpeech\nEmbeddings\n\nLORA\n\nClients\n\nLarge Language Model\n\nAgg = (\n\nclient (1)\n\n(a)\n\n+\n\nclient (2)\n\n+\n\n)/ N\n\nclient (N)\n\n(b)\n\nFig. 1: (a) Conventional Speech-LLM architecture. (b) Proposed federated Speech-LLM training pipeline.\n\nA. SpeechLLM-Based ASR Architecture\n\nC. Large Language Model\n\nAs shown in Figure 1(a), the SpeechLLM architecture integrates three components: a speech encoder, a projection layer,\nand an LLM backbone integrated with LoRA. The speech\nencoder converts raw waveforms into frame-level acoustic\nembeddings. The projection layer then maps these embeddings\ninto the LLM’s token embedding space. Finally, the LLM\nperforms auto-regressive decoding to generate transcriptions.\nFor efficient adaptation, LoRA modules are integrated into the\nLLM layers, allowing only the LoRA and projector parameters\nto be trained while keeping the LLM backbone frozen. In the\nfollowing sections, we briefly explain each component of the\nSpeechLLM pipeline.\n\nIn our pipeline, we rely on TinyLlama-1.1B-Chat-v1.0 [46],\na compact open-source language model with 1.1 billion parameters, trained on approximately 3 trillion tokens following the\nLlama 2 architecture. Despite its small size, it’s specifically\nfine-tuned for conversational interactions using instructionfollowing and chat datasets. The model is designed for efficient\ndeployment on resource-constrained devices while maintaining\nreasonable performance on dialogue tasks, question answering,\nand text generation. TinyLlama uses the same tokenizer and\narchitecture principles as larger Llama models but offers\nsignificantly faster inference and lower memory requirements,\nmaking it practical for edge computing, mobile applications,\nand scenarios where computational resources are limited [47].\nContemporary LLMs are frequently fine-tuned using LoRA\n[48]. LoRA [49] introduces trainable low-rank matrices into\ntransformer layers to approximate weight updates. For a pretrained weight matrix W ∈ Rd×dk , LoRA represents its\nupdate with a low-rank decomposition W+∆W = W+AB,\nwhere A ∈ Rd×r , B ∈ Rr×d are learnable and r ≪ d [50].\n\nB. Speech Encoder\nIn our experiments we investigate the performance of two\ncommon speech encoders ❶ WavLM-large and ❷ Whispermedium.\n❶ WavLM-Large [43] is a self-supervised speech encoder\ndeveloped by Microsoft with 317 million parameters. It uses\nmasked prediction and denoising objectives trained on unlabeled speech data (LibriLight, GigaSpeech, VoxPopuli). The\nmodel excels at extracting robust speech representations that\ncapture both content and speaker characteristics, making it\neffective for downstream tasks like speaker verification, speech\nseparation, and ASR [44]. Its transformer-based architecture\nlearns generalizable features without requiring labeled data.\n❷ Whisper-Medium [45] is OpenAI’s 769-million parameter\nmultilingual speech recognition model trained on 680,000\nhours of weakly-supervised data. It uses an encoder-decoder\ntransformer architecture where the encoder processes melspectrograms and the decoder generates text transcriptions.\nWhisper-Medium supports recognition in 99 languages and\ntranslation to English. It demonstrates strong robustness to diverse accents, background noise, and audio quality variations,\nmaking it practical for real-world ASR applications.\n\nD. Projector\nTo align the dimensionality between the speech encoder and\nlanguage model, we use a simple two-stage linear adapter\nnetwork: a projection layer followed by average pooling\n[51]. The projection layer transforms the speech encoder\nembeddings into the 2048-dimensional input space of TinyLlama. Subsequently, we apply 1D average pooling with a\nkernel size and stride of k = 2 along the time axis, which\ncompresses the sequence length by half. This pooling step\nreduces computational costs during auto-regressive decoding\nwhile consolidating local temporal features into more compact representations. This architecture maintains a minimal\nparameter footprint, concentrating trainable parameters in the\nlinear transformation while the pooling operation provides\nparameter-free compression. During training, only the adapter\nparameters are updated while both the speech encoder and\n\n\flanguage model remain frozen, allowing efficient bridging\nbetween the two pretrained modules.\n\nTABLE I: Statistics (duration in hours, and the number of\nspeakers) of LibriSpeech and MLS datasets.\nLibriSpeech\n\nE. Federated Training Framework\nFigure 1(b) shows the proposed federated learning framework for decentralized SpeechLLM training. Instead of aggregating speech data on a central server, training is performed\ncollaboratively across multiple clients, each holding local\nspeech datasets.\nAt each communication round, the central server broadcasts\nthe current global model parameters to a subset of participating\nclients. Each client performs local training using its private\nspeech data, updating the trainable components of the SpeechLLM (i.e., LoRA and projector layers). The updated local\nmodel parameters are then transmitted back to the server. The\nserver aggregates these parameters using a weighted averaging\nstrategy [52]:\nθ (t+1) =\n\nN\nX\n\nnk\n(t)\nθk\nN\nX\nk=1\nni\n\n(1)\n\ni=1\n\nwhere θ (t+1) denotes the updated global model parameters\n(t)\nat communication round t + 1, θ k represents the locally\nupdated model parameters obtained by client k at round t,\nnk is the number of local training samples at client k, N\nis\nthe total number of participating clients in round t, and\nPN\ni=1 ni represents the total number of training samples across\nall participating clients. The aggregated model is redistributed\nin the next communication round.\nBy restricting parameter updates to lightweight adaptation modules, the proposed framework reduces communication costs and improves scalability when training large\nSpeechLLMs in federated environments. Furthermore, since\nraw speech data never leaves client devices, the framework\nenhances privacy preservation while enabling collaborative\nlearning across geographically distributed users.\nOverall, the proposed architecture bridges SpeechLLM\nmodeling and federated optimization, enabling privacy-aware,\ndecentralized training for end-to-end ASR systems.\nIV. E XPERIMENTAL S ETUP\nA. Dataset\nFor English, we utilize the LibriSpeech-100 dataset (LS)\n[53], consisting of approximately 100 hours of read English\nspeech from LibriVox audiobooks with high-quality, timealigned transcriptions. We use the train-clean-100 split for\ntraining and the standard test-clean set for evaluation. For Italian, we use the Italian portion of the Multilingual LibriSpeech\n(MLS) corpus [54], comprising several hundred hours of read\nItalian speech with corresponding transcriptions from publicdomain LibriVox audiobooks. This dataset enables evaluation\nof multilingual ASR models in a non-English scenario. Table\nI summarizes the statistics of both datasets in terms of hours\nand speakers.\n\nTrain\nTest\n\nMLS\n\nhours\n\n# Spks.\n\nhours\n\n# Spks.\n\n100\n5.4\n\n251\n40\n\n247.38\n5.27\n\n65\n10\n\nB. FedAvg with learning rate scheduler (Adaptive FedAvg)\nThe proposed federated learning strategy introduces key\nenhancements over vanilla FedAvg [52], making it well-suited\nfor large-scale speech model adaptation. Rather than assigning\nstatic and heterogeneous learning rates across client subsets,\nthe modified strategy adopts a unified exponential learning rate\ndecay schedule defined as:\nηt = η0 · γ ⌊t/τ ⌋\n\n(2)\n\nwhere η0 = 0.001 is the initial learning rate, γ = 0.9 is the\ndecay factor, τ = 10 is the decay period in rounds, and t is\nthe current federated round. This promotes training stability\nand smoother convergence, as clients collectively transition\nfrom aggressive early-round updates to more refined late-round\nrefinements.\nC. Federated Learning Setup\nWe implement our FL framework using Flower [55]. We\ndeploy a client for each speaker in the datasets. In each\nround, 30% of the clients are randomly instantiated for 10\nepochs of local training. The resulting gradients are centrally\nagglomerated using FedAvg strategy [52]. FL is applied for\n100 rounds. All FL results marked as —- , —- for LS and\nMLS, respectively, are compared with those achieved with\ncentral training marked as ⋆ , ⋆ for LS and MLS, respectively.\nMore details related to training hyperparameters are publicly\navailable in our GitHub repository 1 .\nV. E XPERIMENTAL R ESULTS\nA. Adaptive FedAvg\nWe conduct a comparative evaluation of both vanilla and\nadaptive FedAvg on the LS dataset, employing WavLM as the\nspeech encoder. Model performance is quantified using WER\nto assess recognition accuracy rigorously.\nThe WER reported in Figure 2 across 100 federated rounds\nconfirm the superiority of the proposed Adaptive FedAvg\nstrategy. At round 20, Adaptive FedAvg achieves a WER of\n9.7% compared to 19.7% for standard FedAvg with a relative\nimprovement of nearly 51%. This demonstrates significantly\nfaster early convergence. By round 100, the proposed strategy\nattains a final WER of 6.4% versus 7.9% for the baseline, approaching the central training reference of 6.1%. These results\nconfirm that the exponential learning rate decay yields both\nfaster convergence and a stronger final model, underscoring\nthe practical value of Adaptive FedAvg for federated speech\nmodel adaptation.\n1 https://github.com/mnabihali/Fed-SpeechLLM\n\n\f100\n\nFedAvg\nAdaptive FedAvg\nCentral Training\n\n100%\n\nWER (%)\n\n80\n60\n\nC. Monolingual FL with Whisper\n\n40\n20\n\n7.9%\n6.4%\n\n0 0\n\nFrom a communication efficiency perspective, both experiments demonstrate that meaningful convergence occurs within\n60-80 federated rounds, with diminishing returns beyond this\npoint. This finding is practically significant, as it establishes a\nreasonable communication budget for federated SpeechLLM\ntraining.\n\n20\n\n40\n\n60\n\nRound\n\n80\n\n6.1%\n\n100\n\nFig. 2: WER comparison between FedAvg and Adaptive\nFedAvg on the LS dataset using WavLM as speech encoder.\nThe dashed line indicates the central training reference (6.1%).\n\nB. Monolingual FL with WavLM\nIn this scenario, we utilize WavLM as a speech encoder for\nthe SpeechLLM architecture for both federated and centralized\napproaches on LS and MLS monolingual datasets, evaluating\nconvergence behavior over 100 federated rounds. Figure 3\npresents the WER trajectories, revealing distinct convergence\npatterns and language-specific challenges in federated speech\nrecognition systems.\nIn the case of the LS dataset, as depicted in Figure 3(a), FL\ndemonstrates remarkable convergence characteristics, starting\nfrom an initial WER of ≈ 100% and rapidly improving to\napproximately 10% by round 20. The steepest performance\ngains occur within the first 20-40 rounds, after which the\nlearning curve stabilizes. By round 100, the FL pipeline\nachieves a WER of 6.4%, effectively matching the centralized\ntraining baseline of approximately 6.1%. This convergence\ndemonstrates that federated optimization can successfully aggregate gradients across distributed clients to reach performance parity with centralized training, despite the challenges\nof non-collocated data and communication constraints.\nRegarding the Italian MLS dataset, as shown in Figure 3(b),\nexhibits more challenging federated learning dynamics. While\nthe convergence pattern follows a similar trajectory starting at\n100% WER and rapidly decreasing through early rounds, a\npersistent performance gap remains throughout training. The\nfederated model achieves approximately 22% WER by round\n100, compared to 20.1% WER for centralized training. This\nabsolute WER difference ≈ 2% highlights the robustness and\nstrong generalization capability of the federated approach,\neven under more challenging data conditions. The languagedependent performance gap between English and Italian federated training highlights the impact of data characteristics on\nfederated optimization effectiveness.\n\nIn this scenario, we investigate the Whisper model as a\nspeech encoder, evaluated under both federated and centralized\noptimization strategies, with the same hyperparameters and\ndatasets as the previous experiment. Figure 4 illustrates the\nWER trends for both monolingual datasets.\nFor LS dataset (Figure 4(a), FL shows rapid convergence,\nreducing WER from 100% to approximately 7% by round\n20. The performance then stabilizes, reaching 6.6% at round\n100, closely matching the performance of the centralized\nbaseline of 6.0%. The small absolute gap of 0.6% demonstrates\nthat federated optimization can effectively preserve Whisper’s\npretrained representations and achieve near-centralized performance.\nFor the MLS Italian dataset (Figure 4(b), convergence\nfollows a similar trend but with a slightly larger gap. The\nfederated model decreases from 100% WER to ≈ 20% within\nthe first 20 rounds and continues improving gradually. By\nround 100, federated training achieves 18.7% WER compared\nto 17.5% under centralized training, resulting in a modest 1.2%\nabsolute difference.\nOverall, the results indicate that Whisper enables stable\nand efficient federated fine-tuning, substantially reducing the\ncentralized–federated performance gap. Moreover, most performance gains occur within the first 40–60 rounds, suggesting\nthat effective convergence can be achieved with a limited\ncommunication budget.\nCompared to WavLM, the Whisper-based federated model\nexhibits greater robustness to client heterogeneity and sustains\na consistently smaller performance gap relative to centralized training, particularly on the MLS dataset. This suggests\nthat Whisper’s pretrained representations and stronger crosslingual generalization capabilities better accommodate the\nstatistical variability inherent in MLS, leading to more stable\nconvergence and enhanced performance under decentralized\noptimization settings.\nD. SpeechLLM versus PEFT\nTable 2 highlights the impact of model parameterization on\noptimization stability and performance under centralized and\nFL using the LS dataset. While full fine-tuning of WavLM\n(85.1M parameters) achieves the best centralized WER (4.4%),\nthis approach requires updating all model parameters and\nis therefore ill-suited for federated settings due to excessive\ncommunication cost and optimization instability. In contrast,\nparameter-efficient methods reduce the number of trainable parameters by over 89% (EL-adapters: 9.1M) and 90% (SpeechLLM: 8.4M) relative to full fine-tuning, while maintaining\ncompetitive recognition accuracy.\n\n\f100\n\n100%\n\nFederated Learning\nCentral Training\n\n60\n40\n20\n20\n\n40\n\n60\n\nRound\n\n80\n\nFederated Learning\nCentral Training\n\n60\n40\n22.6%\n\n20\n\n6.4%\n\n0 0\n\n100%\n\n80\n\nWER (%)\n\n80\n\nWER (%)\n\n100\n\n20.1%\n\n0 0\n\n6.1%\n\n100\n\n20\n\n(a)\n\n40\n\n60\n\nRound\n\n80\n\n100\n\n(b)\n\nFig. 3: WER in Monolingual scenario with SpeechLLM in both central and Federated scenarios using WavLM as speech\nencoder. (a) LS English dataset. (b) MLS Italian dataset.\n\n100\n\n100%\n\nFederated Learning\nCentral Training\n\n60\n40\n20\n0 0\n\n6.6%\n\n20\n\n40\n\n60\n\nRound\n\n80\n\n100%\n\nFederated Learning\nCentral Training\n\n80\n\nWER (%)\n\n80\n\nWER (%)\n\n100\n\n6.0%\n\n100\n\n(a)\n\n60\n40\n18.7%\n\n20\n0 0\n\n17.5%\n\n20\n\n40\n\n60\n\nRound\n\n80\n\n100\n\n(b)\n\nFig. 4: WER in Monolingual scenario with SpeechLLM in both central and Federated scenarios using Whisper as speech\nencoder. (a) LS English dataset. (b) MLS Italian dataset.\n\nIn the federated regime, full WavLM fine-tuning fails\nto converge, confirming the difficulty of optimizing large\nacoustic models under non-IID data distributions and limited client communication. Adapter-based approaches remain\nviable, with federated adapters achieving a WER of 6.1%.\nNotably, Speech-LLM exhibits stable convergence and attains a comparable WER of 6.4% while updating 90.1%\nfewer parameters than WavLM-FT. This demonstrates that\nthe modular speech–language architecture of Speech-LLM is\nwell aligned with federated optimization, enabling effective\nparameter aggregation without sacrificing robustness.\nOverall, these results suggest that LLM-augmented speech\nmodels, when combined with parameter-efficient training\n\nstrategies, can significantly mitigate the communication and\noptimization challenges of federated learning. Speech-LLM\nthus offers a practical trade-off between model expressiveness\nand federated efficiency, narrowing the performance gap between centralized and decentralized ASR while reducing both\ntraining and communication overhead.\nE. From Monolingual to Multilingual\nThis scenario leverages WavLM as the speech encoder using\nall available speakers from both LS and MLS Italian, with a\ntotal of 316 speakers available during training rounds. Figure\n5 illustrates the WER performance across communication\nrounds for both datasets. Starting from an untrained initialization with 100% WER, both datsets demonstrate rapid error\n\n\f100\n\n100%\n\nFederated Learning\nCentral Training\n\n60\n40\n20\n\n16.8%\n\n0 0\n\n20\n\n40\n\n60\n\nRound\n\n6.1%\n\n80\n\n100%\n\nFederated Learning\nCentral Training\n\n80\n\nWER (%)\n\n80\n\nWER (%)\n\n100\n\n100\n\n(a)\n\n60\n40\n19.7%\n\n20\n0 0\n\n18.4%\n\n20\n\n40\n\n60\n\nRound\n\n80\n\n100\n\n(b)\n\nFig. 5: WER in Multilingual scenario with SpeechLLM in both central and Federated scenarios using WavLM as speech\nencoder. (a) LS English dataset. (b) MLS Italian dataset.\nTABLE II: Comparison of centralized and federated training\nsetups using PEFT and SpeechLLM approaches.\nTraining\n\nModel\n\n# Params\n\nWER (%)\n\nCentralized\nTraining\n\nWavLM-FT\nWavLM EL-adapters [8]\nSpeech-LLM\n\n85.1 M\n9.1 M\n8.4 M\n\n4.4\n4.6\n6.1\n\nFederated\nLearning\n\nWavLM\nWavLM EL-adapters [8]\nSpeech-LLM\n\n85.1 M\n9.1 M\n8.4 M\n\n6.1\n6.4\n\nreduction in early rounds followed by gradual convergence.\nBy round 100, the federated model achieves 16.8% WER on\nLS (Figure 5(a)) and 19.7% WER on MLS Italian (Figure\n5(b)). LibriSpeech exhibits greater variability across rounds\nwith notable fluctuations between rounds 20 and 60, while\nMLS Italian demonstrates more stable monotonic convergence.\nCompared to centralized training (6.1% WER on LS and\n18.4.% WER on MLS Italian), the federated approach shows\nperformance gaps of 10.7 percentage points for LibriSpeech\nand a negligible 0.3 percentage points (actually outperforming)\nfor MLS Italian.\nOverall, these results demonstrate effective federated convergence leveraging all available clients, with the multilingual\nmodel achieving near-parity with centralized training on Italian\nwhile maintaining a moderate gap on English.\nVI. C ONCLUSION AND F UTURE W ORK\nThis paper presented a systematic study of federated training\nfor SpeechLLM-based end-to-end ASR in both English and\nItalian monolingual settings. By integrating parameter-efficient\nadaptation (LoRA and projector layers) within a federated\nframework, we demonstrated that large speech–language architectures can be effectively optimized without centralized\n\ndata aggregation. The proposed Adaptive FedAvg strategy\nimproved convergence stability and reduced the performance\ngap between centralized and federated training. Experimental results showed that federated SpeechLLM achieves nearcentralized performance on LibriSpeech and maintains competitive accuracy on MLS Italian, while significantly reducing\ncommunication overhead by updating only a small fraction\nof model parameters. Furthermore, the comparison between\nWavLM and Whisper encoders highlights the importance\nof pretrained multilingual robustness in federated environments. Overall, our findings establish SpeechLLM combined\nwith PEFT as a practical and scalable solution for privacypreserving ASR.\nFuture research will extend this framework toward largescale multilingual and cross-domain federated ASR scenarios\ninvolving more diverse acoustic conditions and highly non-IID\nclient distributions. Incorporating advanced communicationefficient techniques such as gradient compression, adaptive\nclient selection, or hierarchical aggregation could further improve scalability. Additionally, integrating differential privacy\nmechanisms would provide formal privacy guarantees beyond\ndata locality. Exploring personalization strategies, including\nclient-specific adapters or federated continual learning, may\nhelp mitigate heterogeneity and close the remaining centralized federated performance gap. Finally, scaling to larger LLM\nbackbones and evaluating real-world on-device deployment\nconstraints will be essential for practical federated SpeechLLM applications.\nR EFERENCES\n[1] M. U. Hadi et al., “Large language models: a comprehensive survey of\nits applications, challenges, limitations, and future prospects,” Authorea\npreprints, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 1–26, 2023.\n[2] D. Zhang et al., “Mm-llms: Recent advances in multimodal large\nlanguage models,” In Proc. of ACL 2024, pp. 12 401–12 430, 2024.\n\n\f[3] Z. Ma et al., “Speech recognition meets large language model: Benchmarking, models, and exploration,” in Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence, vol. 39, no. 23, 2025, pp. 24 840–24 848.\n[4] J. Yoo et al., “Speechllm: Unified speech and language model for\nenhanced multi-task understanding in low resource settings,” arXiv\npreprint arXiv:2509.04473, 2025.\n[5] S. Alhumoud, “Asr systems using llms a review,” International Jour